    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7233",
      "problem": "Estimate the area under the curve $y=(2x-4)^2+2$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $\\Delta x=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14222967/assets/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(11+6+3+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area.",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14222967/observations/step-e7233-7f2d7e625551719443ae.html",
